facts:
A **truncated axis** — an axis that cuts off part of the scale — can make bar heights give a false impression of relative sizes. For bar-height comparisons, start the value axis at $0$. A nonzero baseline is not automatically misleading for every graph.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.8);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.2,0);
\foreach \v/\p in {26/0,27/1,28/2,29/3,30/4,31/5} {\draw (-0.2,\p)--(0,\p); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\p) {$\v$};}
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.6,4);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (2.0,0) rectangle (3.0,2);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (3.4,0) rectangle (4.4,1);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.1,0) {$A$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,0) {$B$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.9,0) {$C$};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-1.2,2.5) {Value};
\node[below,font=\small] at (2.5,-0.9) {Group};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,5.5) {Axis starts at $26$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **fair bar graph** for comparing values by height starts its value scale at $0$, uses equal steps and equal bar widths, and shows all the relevant data.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,5.2);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.2,0);
\foreach \v/\p in {10/1,20/2,30/3,40/4} {\draw (-0.2,\p)--(0,\p); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\p) {$\v$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (0.6,0) rectangle (1.6,3.0);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (2.0,0) rectangle (3.0,2.8);
\fill[blue!25,draw=black] (3.4,0) rectangle (4.4,2.7);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.1,0) {$A$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,0) {$B$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.9,0) {$C$};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-1.2,2.5) {Value};
\node[below,font=\small] at (2.5,-0.9) {Group};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,4.9) {Same data, axis from $0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

